November 28, 2010.

This is "ggg", a gglcars-based stroboscopic tuner, but redone for
Android.  Given a catchy name "TunerTime" (yay!  it's tuner time!)
because it is eventually destined for release on the Android Market,
where perhaps one of my programs will be used by more than one person.

Hah.

Anyways, I have figured out what I need to do.

android.media.AudioRecord gives me access to the microphone via read().
It is, for example, the interface used by sipdroid (open source sip
phone), so it must be pretty alright at least.  I will call its blocking
read() function over and over in a separate thread I will spawn for just
this purpose.  And that separate thread will have a Surface (or
SurfaceHolder?  I'm not sure) that it will hold onto, and that thread
will somehow copy pixels from the audio data to the Surface (through a
Parcel??).  The rest is simple UI garbage.

A real question, though, is whether it is worth keeping any JNI in this
at all.  The math involved is so simple that it's basically just a big
copy operation.  Though I think honestly that the entire thread could
live from start to finish in C, just always hanging in the JNI method
call to AudioRecord.read(), or into the Surface call to receive a parcel.

I guess I have to design a UI now.  The original has a 3-octave
stroboscopic view surrounded by buttons:
   * QUIT (unneeded on android - user will click 'home' or what-have-you)
   * PAUSE (can be just a tap of the stroboscopic display to pause/unpause, but needs PAUSE notifier!)
   * CONF -> changes other buttons to be +/- for center A (440Hz +/-1Hz
     increments), and +/- for stretch factor (5-digit decimal incremented
     by 25), and RESET (A=440, stretch=0)
   * +/- for octave, semi, and cent.
Plus a status display that provides:
   * A 3     <--- note octave
   * +0c     <--- cents
   * A=440   <--- center A frequency
   * +00005  <--- stretch factor

So I need to do a portrait mode display...

[strobe octave 1] ...
.....................
[strobe octave 2] ...
.....................
[strobe octave 3] ...
.....................
semi+ oct+  cent+ A=440
 A     3     +0c  str=00025
semi- oct-  cent- [CONF]


...

Okay, throwing together the base UI was pretty easy.  It is now fully
functional, except that it doesn't have the thread that does all the
stroboscopic stuff.  So it doesn't do anything, in other words.

Unfortunately I can't really test the stroboscope thread in the emulator.
As near as I have been able to tell from reading, it famously doesn't
have proper microphone support.  But at least the drawing primitives
ought to work, so I can test it with gibberish data.

...

I couldn't get drawing to work at all.  It seems there are supposed to be
two options for drawing from a thread.  They are both through
SurfaceView.  You can then get a SurfaceHolder from that.  From the
SurfaceHolder, you can access the Surface directly, which appears to
allow drawing using "Parcel"s somehow??  Or SurfaceHolder will return a
Canvas (lockCanvas()) object which is supposed to be valid until
unlockCanvasAndPost().  The latter seems more convenient and would allow
us to get rid of the surfaceChanged() et al hooks.  But maybe we should
keep them?  The former seems like it might be more efficient, since it's
visibly part of a performance-conscious IPC stack (so it had better not
introduce extra copying, right?).

Anyways, there is surely something mega dumb and wrong in my code,
because I can't even get it to do a line drawing.


November 29, 2010.

Yesterday I absolutely could not get SurfaceHolder to work.  Today it is
no challenge, though.  I just abandoned my Strobe thread and put together
a little SurfaceHolder thing in the main activity, and that one worked as
I expected it would immediately.  I think the problem is that before one
of the non-lockCanvas() SurfaceHolder calls probably happened in the
Strobe thread instead of the activity thread, causing it to become
corrupted from then on.  Or something.  *shrug*

...

Okay, I'm "done."  And I get this in logcat:
  W/AudioHardwareALSA( 1890): openInputStream : mInput already exists!!

It sounds kind of like the audio device is busy?  An app called
"AudioCaps" doesn't believe my device is capable of recording at all.
Other people say there are all sorts of audio bugs on Samsung devices but
I kind of think they're full of shit.

I recently installed ("Tricorder"), and it was able to read the audio
fine when it was first installed.  But now it cannot.  I suspect it left
the audio device in a bad state.

Indeed, rebooting the device made it get past that hurdle.  Now it seems
to be dying in the JNI, and/or overflowing due to being too slow to init?

Okay, it all came down to logic errors in the way I handled the buffers.
Rather pleasing to see that the moment I got something to display at all,
it was suddenly a perfectly wonderful stroboscopic tuner.  Shows that my
piano is tuned to about 428Hz, which seems plausible.  I will finally
have my tuner with me everywhere I go.  If only I could carry around my
tuning hammer!

A quick check shows that it is consuming about 50% CPU in user-space and
less than 10% in kernel space, so we are probably not CPU constrained,
though it seems like porting the spectrum analyzer over (GGH) will cause
us to really examine that figure a little more closely.


November 24, 2013.

I'd kind of like to release this thing publicly.  I need to take care of
some things.  Like documentation, an icon, and any bug fixes or features
that might be needed.

First, though, I am perplexed by the mis-implementation of stretch.
TunerTime.get_strobe_freq() determines the frequency of the lowest strobe
and honors the stretch.  But for the octave strobes, the stretch factor
is ignored (jni/strobe.c:recalculate() just does * 2.0).  This seems
wrong to me, I think especially the octave strobes should represent the
stretch, so you can tell more easily if you got the right value.

Surprisingly, ggg (the gglcars version) has the same flaw.  However, ggd
(the original fft that grew to have a strobe feature) behaves as I would
expect.  Which supports the idea, because ggd is the one that I actually
used for piano tuning...

